[38;5;231m●[39m I added a [38;5;153mcrit[39m flag to [38;5;153mburst()[39m in [38;5;153mscripts/sparks.gd[39m. [38;5;153mburst(true)[39m now fires twice as many sparks at twice the speed. Plain [38;5;153mburst()[39m works exactly as before, so nothing that
  already calls it needs to change. I didn't run Godot, as you said.

  [2mgdscript[0m
  func burst(crit: bool = false) -> void:
          var mult := CRIT_MULTIPLIER if crit else 1
          amount = _base_amount * mult
          speed_scale = _base_speed_scale * mult
          restart()

  - [1mNormal values come from the scene.[0m In [38;5;153m_ready()[39m the script saves the spark count and speed the node starts with. Each burst works from those saved values, so a crit
    followed by a normal strike goes back to normal instead of doubling again.
  - [1mSpeed-up method:[0m I made the sparks faster with [38;5;153mspeed_scale[39m. That also makes them burn out in half the time. If you'd rather they travel faster but last just as long, I
    can raise their launch velocity instead.
  - [1mEffect on [38;5;153mcool_down()[39m:[0m it still sets the count to 8, but the next burst now resets the count to the scene's starting value. If cool-down is meant to keep later strikes
    smaller, that's worth checking.
  - [1mHooking it up:[0m nothing in the project calls [38;5;153mburst()[39m yet, so the strike code needs to call [38;5;153mburst(true)[39m on a crit.

